@import copy from '../../components/copy.tscl'
@import docs from '../../layouts/docs.tscl'
@import callout from '../../components/callout.tscl'

<docs [title]="'Styles'" [path]="'/guide/styles'" [description]="'Give components their own CSS with a style block scoped with native @scope, and import stylesheets that go through Vite.'">
  <h1>Styles</h1>
  <p class="lead">A component can bring its own CSS in a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block. It is scoped to the component, so its rules don't reach the rest of the page.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim>{{-- components/card.tscl --}}
<section class="card">
  <h2><slot name="title" /></h2>
  <slot />
</section>

<style>
  :scope { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 8px; }
  h2 { margin-top: 0; }
</style></code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>

  <h2>How scoping works</h2>
  <p>clarusjs uses the browser's own <code>@scope</code>: the component's top-level elements are marked with <code>data-cl-c</code>, and its rules apply inside them, stopping at other components.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The top-level element itself</strong> is <code>:scope</code>. A rule like <code>section {}</code> matches elements <em>inside</em> the component, not the top-level one.</li>
    <li><strong>Slot content</strong> sits inside the component, so the component's rules apply to it too.</li>
    <li><strong>Child components</strong> are left alone: their own top-level elements stop the scope.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Global styles</h2>
  <p>Add <code>global</code> to write CSS that isn't scoped, such as custom properties for the whole site.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim><style global>
  :root { --accent: #0b6e70; }
</style></code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>

  <h2>Values from the template</h2>
  <p>A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block is plain CSS: <code verbatim>{{ }}</code> holes aren't allowed in it. Pass values as CSS custom properties instead.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim><div class="bar" style="--width: {{ percent }}%"></div>

<style>
  .bar { width: var(--width); }
</style></code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>

  <h2>Dark mode without a flash</h2>
  <p>A saved theme has to be on the page before it is first drawn, or visitors see the wrong colours for a moment. Put a small <code>&lt;script inline&gt;</code> at the top of your layout's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>: it is written into the page as is and runs before anything is shown. This site does exactly that.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim><head>
  <script inline>
    try {
      const t = localStorage.getItem('theme')
      if (t) document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t)
    } catch {}
  </script>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/site.css">
</head></code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>
  <p>Inline scripts take static attributes only and their content is never treated as a template. On client routes, the router runs the new page's inline head scripts, as a normal page load would.</p>

  <h2>Stylesheets</h2>
  <p>Import a CSS file with <code>@import</code> and no name. It goes through Vite's CSS pipeline (PostCSS or Lightning CSS, minified and hashed), and every page that uses the component links it.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim>{{-- layouts/docs.tscl --}}
@import '../styles/docs.css'</code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>
  <p>Each page links exactly the stylesheets it imports, directly or through its components, in import order with a layout's before the page's own. This site's whole stylesheet is imported that way, by its layout.</p>

  <h2>How styles reach the page</h2>
  <p>A page links its imported stylesheets, then one stylesheet with every component's <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>. With fragment navigation, a page that needs a stylesheet the current page doesn't have gets it before the swap.</p>
  <callout [type]="'info'"><p><code>@scope</code> is supported in all current major browsers. In older ones, scoped rules are ignored.</p></callout>
</docs>
